From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] An application transition waits for the first frame, not five seconds

Tapping an application the owner is logged in to turns the screen into the
status bar and the navigation bar over nothing, and e-paper holds that for
twelve to thirty seconds. The owner reported it as "the WeRead splash screen",
and with it the one fact that located it: "the first time I went in it was
normal."

The deadline is this constant. A transition that replaces one application's
window with another's waits for the incoming window to draw and gives up after
five seconds: WindowManagerService.H.APP_TRANSITION_TIMEOUT calls setTimeout()
and places surfaces anyway, which runs WindowSurfacePlacer's
setVisibility(false) over the closing apps. Measured on the device with
`dumpsys window windows` every two seconds (2026-11-02, v0.1.0, WeRead 2.1.2,
logged in, a dozen books):

    t = 0 s     tap; launcher isOnScreen=true
    t = 2, 4 s  launcher isOnScreen=true; no window for the application yet
    t = 6 s     the application's window is added, mDrawState=DRAW_PENDING
    t = 8 s     unchanged
    t = 11 s    launcher mViewVisibility=0x8 (GONE), isOnScreen=false
                the application is still DRAW_PENDING, and now off screen too

Nothing is left to compose but the two bars, so the composition is black, and
the panel is given it: one GC16 over 823,296 pixels, the same number on five
independent runs. The application draws its first frame twelve to thirty
seconds later, and a second, nearly full-screen GC16 brings the content in.
From the owner's side that is a half-black screen, a long wait, a white flash
and a black flash.

The number. The timer is armed in prepareAppTransitionLocked(), which runs
when the activity is started and not when its window is added, so the eleven
seconds above are where the sampling caught it, not the deadline itself.
Against the timer's own start what has to fit is the whole cold start: WeRead
reaches content 22 to 33 s after `am start` over five runs, and `am start -W`
gives up on its own 15 s wait with `Status: timeout`. 45 s is the slowest of
those runs plus a third again. ro.hw_timeout_multiplier, which this tree
already uses to stretch the not-responding deadlines (patch 0012; 4 on this
product), would give 20 s here and would not cover even the fastest of the
five runs, so this one deadline is taken from the measurement instead of from
that factor.

Why the first time was normal is an inference, not a measurement: with no
account to restore, the application had less to do before its first frame.
The same device on 2026-10-28, before the account was added, showed "a white
screen for a while" rather than a black one. Nothing was sampled on that run,
and nothing rules out some other difference between the two.

Keyguard transitions keep AOSP's five seconds. They are prepared on every
screen-on, including with def_lockscreen_disabled (a PIN set in Settings still
locks), what they wait for is the lock screen and not a cold application
start, and that path behaves as it should today; it was not measured here and
is not changed.

What this does not change:

  * The not-responding deadlines. Input is still 5 s x 4 = 20 s (patch 0012),
    so a tap during a slow start can still raise "not responding" before the
    first frame arrives. The previous screen now stays up over that wait,
    which makes a tap more inviting than a black screen did.
  * What a pending transition defers while it waits: committed visibility,
    window removal, the IME target, the window manager's priority boost. A
    transition that is genuinely wedged is now held for 45 s instead of 5.
  * Who can take input during the wait. The outgoing window has
    hiddenRequested set from the start of the transition, before and after
    this patch, so the launcher is on screen without being the window that
    receives keys.
  * The wait as a whole. Every prepareAppTransitionLocked() removes the
    message and sends it again, so 45 s is the deadline of the transition that
    is pending, not a cap on a run of them.

Not run on the device yet when this patch was written.

--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/AppTransition.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/AppTransition.java
@@ -177,6 +177,16 @@
     private static final int MAX_CLIP_REVEAL_TRANSITION_DURATION = 420;
     private static final int THUMBNAIL_APP_TRANSITION_DURATION = 336;
     private static final long APP_TRANSITION_TIMEOUT_MS = 5000;
+    // PW3: AOSP's five seconds is the deadline for a transition to become
+    // ready -- the incoming window drawn, or a starting window up. A cold
+    // start on this machine reaches its first frame 22 to 33 s after
+    // `am start` (five runs, 2026-11-02, WeRead 2.1.2, logged in), and the
+    // timeout does not cancel the transition, it runs it: the outgoing window
+    // is hidden while the incoming one is still DRAW_PENDING, which leaves the
+    // two system bars over nothing for the rest of the start, and e-paper
+    // holds that. The slowest of those runs plus a third again, so that the
+    // previous screen stays until there is something to replace it with:
+    private static final long PW3_APP_TRANSITION_TIMEOUT_MS = 45000;
 
     private final Context mContext;
     private final WindowManagerService mService;
@@ -2071,7 +2081,14 @@
         boolean prepared = prepare();
         if (isTransitionSet()) {
             mService.mH.removeMessages(H.APP_TRANSITION_TIMEOUT);
-            mService.mH.sendEmptyMessageDelayed(H.APP_TRANSITION_TIMEOUT, APP_TRANSITION_TIMEOUT_MS);
+            // PW3: a transition into an application waits for that application's
+            // first frame, which takes tens of seconds here. A Keyguard
+            // transition waits for the lock screen instead and keeps AOSP's
+            // deadline; it is prepared on every screen-on, and that path works
+            // as it is.
+            mService.mH.sendEmptyMessageDelayed(H.APP_TRANSITION_TIMEOUT,
+                    isKeyguardTransit(mNextAppTransition)
+                            ? APP_TRANSITION_TIMEOUT_MS : PW3_APP_TRANSITION_TIMEOUT_MS);
         }
         return prepared;
     }
